---
import FaqLanding from '../../components/FaqLanding.astro';

const faqs = [
	{
		q: 'Our bank is already supervised by the BSP. Do we also answer to the NPC?',
		a: 'Yes — they cover different things. The <strong>BSP</strong> regulates you as a financial institution (MORB, IT risk, consumer protection). The <strong>National Privacy Commission (NPC)</strong> regulates how you collect and handle <em>personal data</em> under the Data Privacy Act (RA 10173). A BSP-compliant bank can still be penalised by the NPC. The two overlap heavily, so your privacy program should be built to satisfy both at once.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Does a bank legally need a Data Protection Officer?',
		a: 'Yes — and so does every other organization that processes personal data. IRR Section 26(a) and NPC Advisory No. 2017-01 apply the duty to <strong>any</strong> person or body processing personal data, with no threshold and no exemption for size. For a bank the point is what sits beneath it: the government-issued IDs in your KYC files are sensitive personal information under DPA Section 3(l)(3), and credit scoring is profiling — which must be registered at any size. The DPO is also the registered contact the NPC deals with.',
	},
	{
		q: 'What happens if customer financial data is breached?',
		a: 'A breach of financial data carries some of the highest stakes under the law: account numbers, balances, and IDs enable fraud directly. You must assess and, where required, notify the NPC and affected clients within <strong>72 hours</strong> of knowledge of the breach. Late or missing notification is itself a violation — on top of the breach.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Do we have to register with the NPC, and how often?',
		a: 'Almost certainly yes. Registration is triggered by any one of four grounds in Circular 2022-04 Section 5 — 250 or more persons employed, sensitive personal information on 1,000 or more individuals, processing likely to pose a risk to rights and freedoms, or automated decision-making and profiling at any size. Credit scoring puts most banks over the last one on its own. Renewal is only within the 30 days before the certificate expires. We handle the eligibility assessment, the filing, and keeping it current so it does not lapse.',
	},
	{
		q: 'We use third-party processors (core banking, e-KYC, collections). Are we still liable?',
		a: 'Yes. As the Personal Information Controller, the bank stays accountable for data handled by vendors. That liability has to be managed through proper data-sharing and outsourcing agreements and vendor due diligence — which the DPO oversees.',
	},
	{
		q: 'What can NPC penalties actually cost us?',
		a: 'Two separate regimes. NPC <strong>administrative</strong> fines run to 0.5%–3% of your prior-year annual gross income for a grave infraction and 0.25%–2% for a major one, capped at ₱5 million for any single act; failure to register is ₱50,000–₱200,000 per infraction. The DPA’s <strong>criminal</strong> provisions are separate and fall on responsible officers personally — 6 months to 7 years depending on the offence. Add BSP scrutiny under RA 11765 on top: the penalties stack, they do not replace each other. For a bank, the cost of a compliant privacy program is a fraction of a single enforcement action.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Can you act as our external DPO?',
		a: 'Yes. As a TÜV Certified DPO (Cert #7000532) based in Region 8, I can serve as your designated external DPO, build your Privacy Management Program, train staff, and be your point of contact with the NPC — without the cost of a full-time hire.',
	},
];
